<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    AuditLog's cross-section surface, carved into its own leaf project because almost every
    consumer is in Base: ~20 Humans.Application services (Users, Profiles, Camps, Shifts,
    GoogleIntegration, Auth, HumanLifecycle), three Humans.Infrastructure jobs, and Shell's
    controllers and seeder. A Contracts/ *folder* inside Humans.AuditLog would therefore make
    Base reference a section and cycle (design §15.5b,
    memory/architecture/section-project-cycle-fix.md).

    The section project also has a Contracts/ folder, for IAuditViewerService / AuditEvent /
    AuditEventPage — the read+render surface, whose consumers are all Shell or other sections
    and can ProjectReference Humans.AuditLog directly (G5 lane 4b-2h). Both share the
    Humans.AuditLog.Contracts namespace, as Shifts and Tickets already do. The rule that
    decides which home a type gets: a leaf member needs an out-of-section consumer that
    cannot reach the section project.

    The surface here is deliberately the whole of IAuditLogService: the append path is called
    from ~130 files across every section, and the read members it also carries are the raw
    entry queries AuditViewerService wraps. Notifications' "a wide fan-in over a narrow
    interface is cheap; it is the surface that costs" holds — this is one interface and one
    record.

    AuditAction lives here. G5 lane 4b-2l re-measured the claim that stood in this comment
    — that it was Base vocabulary pinned by "a Humans.UI renderer is a hard floor on how far
    an enum can move" — and both halves are false: no Humans.UI file names AuditAction, and
    4b-2k had already reversed that rule (Humans.UI reaches every leaf through
    Humans.Application). AuditLog is the only section that writes it to a table, so it is
    this section's vocabulary; its ~120 consumers all reach this leaf already, because they
    reach it to call IAuditLogService.

    References Humans.Interfaces (IApplicationService) and Humans.Domain: GoogleSyncSource is
    still Base vocabulary named in these signatures and stays in Humans.Domain.Enums.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Humans.Interfaces\Humans.Interfaces.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Humans.Domain\Humans.Domain.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="NodaTime" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
